   124	### Workspace, path dependency and L4
   125	
   126	**Decision proposed for Claude:** do not create a shared workspace or a root path dependency in Task 1.1. The root `Cargo.toml` has no `[workspace]`, no engine dependency, and root `src/` has no engine consumer. A nested independent Cargo package needs neither a root workspace entry nor a root dependency. Its own lockfile and explicit `--manifest-path` commands give a separate feature resolution. Keep root manifest/lock/source byte-identical.
   127	
   128	Task 1.2 lives inside the engine package. Task 1.3's planned `src/kaggriculture/observe.rs` may become the first root consumer when compiled against `Game`; Task 1.4 certainly will. Add the path dependency at the **first actual compiled consumer**, after reviewing that task, rather than claiming nothing before 1.4 can need it. There is no consumer that justifies it in **this** task.
   129	
   130	Workspace membership is not a dependency edge, but it is also **not feature isolation**. Cargo's locally installed primary documentation (`share/doc/rust/html/cargo/reference/resolver.html`, “Features”, in `nightly-2026-04-18-aarch64-apple-darwin`) explicitly states that selecting multiple workspace packages with `--workspace` or multiple `-p` flags unifies dependency features; separate invocations avoid that unification. A shared workspace also shares lock resolution. Resolver 2/3 does not isolate a normal engine dependency from the root's active test dependency on the same `serde_json` package. A root-only default invocation can avoid the engine when no dependency selects it, but `cargo test --workspace` would reintroduce L4.
   131	
   132	Keep engine `serde_json` features `arbitrary_precision` and `preserve_order`: removing them breaks the kernel's tested unbounded integer/numeric and ordered-inventory semantics. If Claude chooses a shared workspace now, the minimal way to keep unmodified root decoding is **separate package test invocations** (never selecting both); the more robust choice is the standalone package proposed here. Once the root needs the path dependency, apply the reference's narrow test-only repair, not feature deletion:
   133	
   134	```rust
   135	// src/rules_engine/generation.rs, test RandomCall::Uniform only:
   136	Uniform {
   137	    #[serde(deserialize_with = "fixture_float")]
   138	    low: f64,
   139	    #[serde(deserialize_with = "fixture_float")]
   140	    high: f64,
   141	    #[serde(deserialize_with = "fixture_float")]
   142	    value: f64,
   143	},
   144	
   145	fn fixture_float<'de, D: serde::Deserializer<'de>>(deserializer: D) -> Result<f64, D::Error> {
   146	    serde_json::Number::deserialize(deserializer)?
   147	        .as_f64()
   148	        .filter(|number| number.is_finite())
   149	        .ok_or_else(|| serde::de::Error::custom("expected a finite fixture number"))
   150	}
   151	```
   152	
   153	This handles Serde's tagged-enum buffering of arbitrary-precision decimals as Number's private map. The exact reference diff is obtainable with `git diff HEAD kg/reference-2026-09-29 -- src/rules_engine/generation.rs`. It is **not applied in Task 1.1**. Reopen L4 when adding a root dependency/workspace; test the affected generation fixtures red before the decoder repair, then require 155 passed / 2 ignored unchanged.
